Process Management in Operating System:

1. What is a Process?
A process is a program in execution.

A program is a passive entity (set of instructions), whereas a process is an active entity (with program counter, stack, data, etc.).

A process is the basic unit of CPU scheduling and resource management.
------------------------------------
2. Components of a Process
Every process consists of:

Text Section (Code) → Instructions to be executed.

Data Section → Global variables.

Heap → Dynamically allocated memory during runtime.

Stack → Function calls, local variables, return addresses.

Program Counter (PC) → Address of next instruction.

Process Control Block (PCB) → Metadata of process (explained below).
---------------------------------
3. Process Control Block (PCB)
Each process is represented by a PCB in the OS.
The PCB stores all essential information about a process:

Process ID (PID)

Process State (running, waiting, etc.)

Program Counter

CPU Registers

Memory Management Info (base, limit, page tables)

Scheduling Info (priority, scheduling queues)

I/O Status Info (open files, devices)

👉 PCB acts as the identity card of a process.
------------------------------------
4. Process States
A process moves through multiple states during execution:

New → Process is being created.

Ready → Waiting for CPU allocation.

Running → Currently executing on CPU.

Waiting/Blocked → Waiting for I/O or event.

Terminated → Finished execution.

Some systems also add: Suspended Ready and Suspended Blocked.

📌 State Transition Diagram is often used to show movement between states.
----------------------------------
5. Process Scheduling
Since CPU is limited, the OS uses scheduling to decide which process runs next.

Types of Schedulers:
Long-term Scheduler (Job Scheduler)

Decides which jobs enter the system.

Controls degree of multiprogramming.

Medium-term Scheduler

Suspends/resumes processes.

Improves performance by reducing load.

Short-term Scheduler (CPU Scheduler)

Decides which ready process runs on CPU.

Runs frequently (milliseconds).
-------------------------------------
6. Scheduling Queues
Processes are managed using queues:

Job Queue → All processes in the system.

Ready Queue → All processes ready for CPU.

Device Queue → Processes waiting for I/O devices.
-----------------☆☆☆☆
7. Context Switching
When CPU switches from one process to another:

Current process state is saved in its PCB.

New process state is loaded from its PCB.

👉 Context switching is overhead (no useful work, but essential for multitasking).
