🔹 2. Multitasking
Meaning: Multiple tasks (programs or processes) run apparently at the same time by fast switching.

Goal: Improve responsiveness for users.

Example: Listening to music while writing code and downloading files.

Note: It’s a form of multiprogramming with time-sharing.

🔹 3. Multiprocessing
Meaning: A system with two or more CPUs (cores) executes multiple processes in true parallelism.

Goal: Boost performance and reliability.

Example: Multi-core processors running multiple apps simultaneously.

🔹 4. Multithreading
Meaning: A single process is divided into multiple threads that run concurrently, sharing memory.

Goal: Perform multiple subtasks simultaneously within one application.

Example: A web browser – one thread for rendering, one for input, one for network.

Benefit: Faster execution, less memory usage than multiple processes.

🔹 5. Distributed Processing
Meaning: Computation is split across multiple networked systems working together.

Goal: Scalability, fault tolerance, and performance.

Example: Hadoop cluster, Kubernetes, or microservices on cloud nodes.

🔹 6. Time-Sharing
Meaning: Each process gets a fixed CPU time slice, then the CPU switches to another process.

Goal: Fair CPU allocation among multiple users or processes.

Example: Multi-user Unix/Linux systems.

Note: Basis for multitasking in modern OSes.

🔹 7. Real-Time Processing
Meaning: System guarantees completion of tasks within strict time constraints.

Goal: Predictability and reliability in time-sensitive operations.

Example: Airbag system, flight control, pacemakers.

Types:

Hard real-time: Missing a deadline = system failure.

Soft real-time: Occasional misses acceptable.

🔹 8. Concurrency
Meaning: Managing multiple tasks at once, not necessarily executing simultaneously.

Goal: Structure systems to handle many overlapping operations.

Example: Handling thousands of simultaneous HTTP requests via async I/O.

🔹 9. Parallelism
Meaning: Actually running multiple tasks at the same time using multiple CPUs/cores.

Goal: Speed up computation.

Example: Matrix multiplication on GPU cores.

